1
00:00:00,109 --> 00:00:03,999
This is CueMend: a browser
agent repairs caption timing

2
00:00:03,999 --> 00:00:07,163
while a human protects the performance.

3
00:00:07,163 --> 00:00:09,593
Here is the result first—zero conflicts

4
00:00:09,593 --> 00:00:12,997
and a verifiable, editable export.

5
00:00:13,756 --> 00:00:16,566
We reset to a fictional
32-second rehearsal

6
00:00:16,566 --> 00:00:20,960
with ten captions and seven
independent timing problems.

7
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:26,694
Automation can measure overlap,
reading rate, and visual collisions.

8
00:00:26,694 --> 00:00:29,239
It cannot decide whether
an audience laugh,

9
00:00:29,239 --> 00:00:33,338
lighting reveal, or actor's
breath deserves silence.

10
00:00:33,338 --> 00:00:36,718
Those decisions stay
in the human interface.

11
00:00:37,477 --> 00:00:40,562
Through Chrome's native WebMCP channel,

12
00:00:40,562 --> 00:00:44,946
the agent inspects the live
revision, audits the active track,

13
00:00:44,946 --> 00:00:47,936
and calls CueMend's staging tool.

14
00:00:47,936 --> 00:00:50,555
The browser deterministically
evaluates all

15
00:00:50,555 --> 00:00:54,654
six thousand five hundred
sixty-one authored combinations,

16
00:00:54,654 --> 00:00:58,554
then exposes three
zero-conflict alternatives.

17
00:00:58,554 --> 00:01:02,063
Staging never mutates the active captions.

18
00:01:02,822 --> 00:01:05,712
The first live preview minimizes motion,

19
00:01:05,712 --> 00:01:09,251
but it crosses Jon's
optional final breath.

20
00:01:09,251 --> 00:01:13,501
The stage manager protects that
beat with one visible click.

21
00:01:13,501 --> 00:01:16,055
The workspace moves to revision two,

22
00:01:16,055 --> 00:01:21,414
the conflict count rises to eight,
and the old proposal becomes stale.

23
00:01:22,173 --> 00:01:25,528
When the agent retries
its revision-one request,

24
00:01:25,528 --> 00:01:29,487
CueMend returns stale
revision and changes nothing.

25
00:01:29,487 --> 00:01:34,736
The capability inventory also
retires unsafe proposal tools.

26
00:01:34,736 --> 00:01:40,200
The agent must inspect the current state
and replan under the new human constraint.

27
00:01:40,959 --> 00:01:45,419
The new search finds two
hundred fifty-six legal tracks.

28
00:01:45,419 --> 00:01:47,744
The agent previews plan one,

29
00:01:47,744 --> 00:01:51,098
but only the human can
select and approve it.

30
00:01:51,098 --> 00:01:54,747
Approval binds the exact
proposal, plan, revision,

31
00:01:54,747 --> 00:01:58,242
and SHA-256 digest.

32
00:01:58,242 --> 00:02:01,751
Only then does the one-shot
commit tool appear.

33
00:02:02,510 --> 00:02:07,989
The agent invokes that temporary commit
capability with an idempotency key.

34
00:02:07,989 --> 00:02:13,103
CueMend revalidates the approval and
beat locks before changing anything.

35
00:02:13,103 --> 00:02:17,818
The shared page immediately shows
revision three, zero conflicts,

36
00:02:17,818 --> 00:02:21,727
a clean cue rail, and
an immutable receipt.

37
00:02:21,727 --> 00:02:24,372
Mutation tools disappear.

38
00:02:25,131 --> 00:02:28,921
Finally, the agent independently
re-audits the committed track

39
00:02:28,921 --> 00:02:34,920
and exports editable WebVTT plus a
limitations certificate. The certificate

40
00:02:34,920 --> 00:02:39,169
proves this exact fictional fixture
under the named demo profile;

41
00:02:39,169 --> 00:02:43,563
it does not claim universal
accessibility compliance.

42
00:02:43,563 --> 00:02:46,722
CueMend lets the agent do
exhaustive mechanical work

43
00:02:46,722 --> 00:02:49,267
while the human keeps the beat.
